proof.text:
Write coordinates in the chosen rational basis, and let $U=s_s$, $T=s_t$. For $(a,b)=(0,0)$ they act by $U(x,y)=(-x,y)$, $T(x,y)=(x,-y)$, so the union of the two unit-vector orbits is exactly $\{\pm(1,0),\pm(0,1)\}$. For $(a,b)=(-m,-1)$ their formulas are $U(x,y)=(-x+my,y)$ and $T(x,y)=(x,x-y)$. Define positive lists $P_1=((1,0),(0,1),(1,1))$, $P_2=((1,0),(0,1),(1,1),(2,1))$, $P_3=((1,0),(0,1),(1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(3,2))$. Substitution into the two formulas gives the following image lists, in the same order as the inputs:
$m=1$: $U(P_1)=((-1,0),(1,1),(0,1))$, $T(P_1)=((1,1),(0,-1),(1,0))$;
$m=2$: $U(P_2)=((-1,0),(2,1),(1,1),(0,1))$, $T(P_2)=((1,1),(0,-1),(1,0),(2,1))$;
$m=3$: $U(P_3)=((-1,0),(3,1),(2,1),(1,1),(0,1),(3,2))$, $T(P_3)=((1,1),(0,-1),(1,0),(2,1),(3,2),(3,1))$.
Linearity gives the images of the negatives. Thus $P_m\cup(-P_m)$ is stable under both involutions and contains both unit vectors, so every word in $U,T$ applied to either unit vector belongs to this list.
